      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around the Oregon Railroad and Navigation Company, which built the Union Pacific line through the area in the 1870s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermiston%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The historic Hinkle Terminal and hump yard just outside Hermiston house Union Pacific's largest locomotive repair facility.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermiston%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hermiston’s economy exploded in the 1970s when cheap power and center‑pivot irrigation turned the area into a leading potato producer, attracting plants from Lamb‑Weston and Simplot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermiston%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Umatilla County Historical Society Museum in Hermiston preserves artifacts from the Umatilla, Cayuse, Walla Walla, and Columbia Indians, the region’s earliest inhabitants.",
